# CODEOWNERS — the judging surface
#
# WHAT THIS DOES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
#
# It auto-requests review from the owner on any PR touching a path below, and shows
# ownership in the PR UI. That is visibility and attribution.
#
# It does **not** block a merge. `master`'s ruleset sets
# required_approving_review_count = 0, so no review is required at all — and turning
# code-owner review on would deadlock a solo maintainer, because GitHub will not let a
# PR author satisfy their own code-owner review. Enabling it needs a second reviewer
# to exist first. Recorded here so nobody mistakes this file for a gate.
#
# WHY THESE PATHS
#
# The 2026-08-19 → 08-25 autonomous run drifted, and every drift was the same trade:
# throughput bought by weakening a check. A canary case not written. An acceptance
# criterion not tested. A CI step deleted to unblock a queue. Individually small, and
# each one removed a mechanism that existed to catch the class of error that followed.
#
# The common factor is that **the things doing the judging were editable by the thing
# being judged.** These paths are that judging layer. An agent may still change them —
# nothing here prevents it — but the change arrives with a review request attached
# rather than inside a 400-line diff nobody reads.
#
# Everything else in the repo is deliberately unowned. Requesting review on every PR
# would train whoever reads them to stop reading.

# ── CI and the checks themselves ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# ci.yml carries the gates; monitors.yml reads the live chain and must never be
# reachable from a pull_request (CC-085).
/.github/                       @Wahzammo

# ── The test harness's own guarantees ────────────────────────────────────────
# vitest.setup.ts is what makes the suite hermetic — it strips signing keys and blocks
# the network (CC-060). A quiet edit here re-enables a suite that can broadcast real
# transactions. vitest.config.ts carries the timeout that stops speed-dependent flake.
/vitest.setup.ts                @Wahzammo
/vitest.config.ts               @Wahzammo

# ── The checker's fixture set ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The canary set is the only thing that can tell whether the checker is *right* rather
# than merely running. `phash_max_similarity_to` was inverted for five days because it
# was the one criterion with no failing case here (ADR-0001 A3.4).
/src/lib/checker/canary/        @Wahzammo
/src/lib/checker/__tests__/     @Wahzammo

